WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:05.750
Google, Adobe, Microsoft, and IBM are all
run by people who grew up in India.

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:10.280
The latest to join these ranks is Twitter’s
CEO Parag Agrawal who took over from Jack

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:11.519
Dorsey.

00:00:11.519 --> 00:00:17.370
People originally from India make up 1% of
the total U.S. population yet about 6% of

00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:18.850
Silicon Valley’s workforce.

00:00:18.850 --> 00:00:22.850
So, why are there so many tech CEOS who hail
from India?

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:28.830
When Satya Nadella took over as CEO of Microsoft
in 2014, the company was in bad shape.

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:31.960
Bill Gates was reportedly an angry and difficult
boss.

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:35.460
His successor, Steve Ballmer, apparently wasn’t
any better.

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:37.250
Microsoft was also a dinosaur.

00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:38.910
It had failed to innovate.

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:42.310
For example, it lost the smartphone battle
to Apple.

00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:46.550
Along comes Nadella, an engineer who rose
through the ranks of Microsoft to become the

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:47.550
CEO.

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:50.130
Nadella changed the culture of the company.

00:00:50.130 --> 00:00:53.190
He made it clear anger and yelling weren’t
welcome.

00:00:53.190 --> 00:00:55.410
He also sought to make Microsoft relevant.

00:00:55.410 --> 00:01:01.710
We now need to make Microsoft thrive in a
mobile-first and cloud-first world.

00:01:01.710 --> 00:01:06.740
Under his direction, Microsoft moved its software
to non-Windows devices and expanded its cloud

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:08.210
business Azure.

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:13.350
That innovative spirit of problem-solving
was bred in his native country.

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:16.550
India is home to nearly 1.4 billion people.

00:01:16.550 --> 00:01:21.020
It’s the second-most populous country on
earth, after China, and is expected to overtake

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:24.450
China as the most populous country in a few
years.

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:29.510
Although India has made remarkable progress,
poverty is still a major challenge.

00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:34.600
Nearly a quarter of the population lives below
the poverty line of $1.90 a day according

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:36.630
to the United Nations Development Programme.

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:41.140
They face corruption, poor infrastructure,
and limited opportunities.

00:01:41.140 --> 00:01:45.390
So they learn to be resilient, adapt, and
overcome endless obstacles.

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:50.380
This leads them to be problem-solvers - a
key asset in any corporation.

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:55.050
Sundar Pichai has had to solve many problems
after taking over at the helm of Google in

00:01:55.050 --> 00:02:00.280
2015…at a time when big tech is being scrutinized
over the power they hold.

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:04.810
He became the archetype of a kinder, calmer,
gentler leader in tech.

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:09.259
Pichai earned an engineering degree at one
of the Indian Institutes of Technology.

00:02:09.259 --> 00:02:14.640
The IITs are kind of like the Indian equivalent
of MIT with an even lower acceptance rate

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.340
reportedly of 1% or 2%.

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:22.700
After Pichai graduated, his ticket to America
came in the form of a Master’s at Stanford.

00:02:22.700 --> 00:02:27.650
Going to a top grad school in the States is
how many Indians land up in the land of opportunity.

00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:30.209
They appear to follow this well-trodden path:

00:02:30.209 --> 00:02:32.800
They did their undergrad at a great school
in India.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:34.280
Got a Master’s in the U.S.

00:02:34.280 --> 00:02:39.190
Where they studied STEM - science, technology,
engineering, and mathematics.

00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:43.420
More than half of all international students
in America pursued a STEM degree in the past

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:44.970
academic year.

00:02:44.970 --> 00:02:48.610
American immigration favors people with specialized
skills.

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:53.980
After Pichai graduated, he went on an H1B
visa to work at consulting firm McKinsey.

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:59.470
The H1B allows American companies to employ
foreign workers, many of whom are in IT-related

00:02:59.470 --> 00:03:00.470
fields.

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:06.489
The U.S. government awarded nearly 75% of
these visas to Indians in 2020.

00:03:06.489 --> 00:03:10.450
In comparison, China trailed far behind in
second, at 12%.

00:03:10.450 --> 00:03:15.060
The fact that so many Indians can speak English
is a tremendous advantage in the West.

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:19.670
And they’re comfortable with American business
culture, possibly because it mirrors the thriving

00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:23.560
IT industry back home in Bangalore, Chennai,
and Hyderabad.

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:26.810
They’re also more likely to want to work
overseas.

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:32.250
80% of Chinese international students return
to China after graduating, according to China’s

00:03:32.250 --> 00:03:34.150
Ministry of Education.

00:03:34.150 --> 00:03:37.650
Chinese entrepreneurs prefer starting their
own companies at home.

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:42.700
Alibaba is known as the “Amazon of China”
Tencent owns the messaging app WeChat used

00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:45.060
by 1.2 billion people.

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:48.500
Huawei is one of the giants of the tech industry.

00:03:48.500 --> 00:03:53.030
China's transition from a manufacturing hub
to a tech hotbed has largely been driven by

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:54.560
government policy.

00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:58.720
In the late seventies, Chinese leader Deng
Xiaoping encouraged foreign businesses to

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:02.849
set up offices in China which fostered a start-up
environment.

00:04:02.849 --> 00:04:07.760
On the other hand, India has a problem of
too much talent leaving the country for good.

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:13.040
It has the highest number of migrants living
abroad at 17.5 million, according to the UN.

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:18.380
And more Indians gave up their passports to
become naturalized American citizens in 2020

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:21.729
than the Chinese, continuing the trend from
past years.

00:04:21.729 --> 00:04:25.880
However, the brain drain from India may be
starting to recede.

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:31.120
An increasing number of homegrown Indian startups
are becoming unicorns - meaning, the companies

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:33.680
are valued at $1 billion or more.

00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:40.400
The data platform Tracxn tells
Newsthink India created 47 unicorns in 2021.

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:43.771
A significant jump from the 17 in 2020.

00:04:43.771 --> 00:04:45.849
And even less in years past.

00:04:45.849 --> 00:04:50.729
The tech ecosystem is being bolstered by powerful
investment firms such as Softbank that have

00:04:50.729 --> 00:04:53.180
been pumping money into Indian startups.

00:04:53.180 --> 00:04:57.159
But in order to entice more people to remain
in their native country, salaries will have

00:04:57.159 --> 00:04:59.250
to catch up with those in the West.

00:04:59.250 --> 00:05:04.460
The average American tech worker earns around
$98,000 a year according to the online recruiting

00:05:04.460 --> 00:05:06.050
platform, Dice.

00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:10.669
Tech salaries in India vary greatly, but the
jobs site Indeed pegs the median tech salary

00:05:10.669 --> 00:05:16.660
at $20,000 US going up to as much as $33,000
which makes them some of the top earners in

00:05:16.660 --> 00:05:20.819
their country but still, far less than what
they’d make in the States.

00:05:20.819 --> 00:05:25.050
As salaries grow along with India’s startup
boom, this could have implications for the

00:05:25.050 --> 00:05:29.460
number of exceptional individuals desiring
to move abroad.

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:33.529
The one thing that the CEOs of tech giants
have in common is their strong background

00:05:33.529 --> 00:05:34.669
in engineering.

